>So I play a game last week, one of my best, and when it's over, find that, 
>not only have I not made the scoreboard, but the entire top ten list has 
>been consumed by this other guy's character!  And his scores beat mine by a 
>MILE!  All well and good, I say to myself, after the initial shock.  I should
>talk to this guy, looks like he's a pretty damn good nethack player.
>
>Then I notice:  the bastard is pulling the old save-rename-recover trick!  By
>the opinions of myself and at least a few others here, that's out and out 
>cheating!  So, the ethical dilemma I pose to you is such:

The issue isn't so much whether it's "ethical" to pull the save files trick (or
even if there are any ethics at all when it comes to a non-competitive game),
but rather whether it's cool for someone to clobber a public high-scores list
with 50 instances of the same character.

The answer here is obviously "no".  By doing this, he is preventing someone
else from getting to record his scores.  Possibly even wiping out a large
number of those scores by shoving them off the bottom of the list.  And, he is
making for a very uninteresting record file!

What I'd do is this : edit the record file, removing all that character's
scores except the lowest (where he died the first time).  If PC's still create
a logfile (dunno if they do anymore), you could even add back other scores that
may have been pushed off the bottom of the top 100.

Explain to the guy that he's screwing up the game, and ask him to a) back up
the record file before he ressurects himself, then restore it when he's done;
or b) play in discovery mode, which allows him to live forever anyway, and
doesn't put those scores in the list.

For me, one of the best parts about hack is the list of interesting character
names, and the weird and varied ways they bought the farm.  Anything that takes
away from that aspect isn't a good thing.  BTW, someone posted a perl script to
make a monster high scores list out of your normal high scores list - finally
got perl installed, it works great, and is quite fun!  It seems that Dwarves
(followed by Gnomes) are among the most sucessful monsters onmy machines.

